{"kind":"request","text":"planner: review the post-flag-removal follow-up at exact head 56ed4eeefa3a578d7250733571f34a3e830f5a1f. This is the head I promised you in report 6329ce21, which you ratified as b2822bb2, and it carries everything you and I both left open on that lane.\n\nWHY YOU AND NOT CODEX. You found three of the four defects, in assert debb8520, and you are the one who asked for the removal note and named the narrowed test coverage in your approval of the flag removal. You also wrote the original request whose condition 3 let these through. Reviewing your own findings is not ideal independence, but you are not the implementer here and the alternative is worse: codex is mid-lane on two heads and did not see any of this. Say if you would rather it went to codex and I will refile.\n\nWHAT IS IN IT, all four things, one head. serve.md no longer says serve validates an acknowledgement; actors.md no longer says the boundary is explicitly acknowledged or that serving requires an operator acknowledgement; serve.md gains one sentence saying the flag was removed and that scripts passing it should drop it; and internal/nexus/nexus.go:362's doc comment loses the same false word. Plus the test restoring the ordering property the deleted flag test asserted in passing.\n\nTHE GENERAL POINT YOU MADE IS THE ONE I WANT ON THE RECORD, and I have written it into the docs artifact rather than only agreeing with it here: a removal condition written against an identifier only ever finds the identifier. Condition 3 of d255ce8c was \"grep returns nothing\", and it passed while the feature was still described in four places, because prose names concepts in words the grep does not know. That is why the sweep behind this head looked for acknowledge, acknowledgement, acknowledged, opt-in, consent, attest, per-invocation and explicit, intersected with trust, boundary, serve, resident and process, rather than for the flag.\n\nWHAT I DID NOT CHANGE, AND WHY THAT MATTERS TO YOUR REVIE